    ('q-pr89-rwfib-06-01', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-rw', 'Fill in the Blanks (Dropdown)',
     'Insect Aerodynamics: Steady-State Paradox', 'Below is a text with blanks. Click on each blank, a list of choices will appear. Select the appropriate answer choice for each blank.',
     'For decades, classical aeronautical theory struggled to explain insect flight. Standard aerodynamic equations, which model passenger aircraft, assumed that air flows smoothly over rigid wings. At miniature scales, however, air behaves as a [[b1]] fluid, and insect wings undergo rapid oscillations. By discarding stationary wing models, scientists recognized that insects rely on [[b2]] aerodynamics. This dynamic approach finally [[b3]] the long-standing flight paradox.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["frictionless", "porous", "rarefied", "viscous"], "correctAnswer": "viscous"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["laminar", "quiescent", "static", "unsteady"], "correctAnswer": "unsteady"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["exacerbated", "obfuscated", "postponed", "resolved"], "correctAnswer": "resolved"}]'::jsonb,
     'b1: ''viscous'' matches air behaving like thick fluid at micro-scales (''porous'' means permeable; ''frictionless'' is opposite; ''rarefied'' means thin). b2: ''unsteady'' describes dynamic non-steady aerodynamics (''static'', ''laminar'', and ''quiescent'' mean steady or still). b3: ''resolved'' means solving the paradox (''obfuscated'' means confusing; ''exacerbated'' means worsening; ''postponed'' means delaying).', 3, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-rwfib', 1, TRUE, 150, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-rwfib-06-02', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-rw', 'Fill in the Blanks (Dropdown)',
     'Flight Musculature: Thoracic Mechanics', 'Below is a text with blanks. Click on each blank, a list of choices will appear. Select the appropriate answer choice for each blank.',
     'Insects possess two distinct muscular designs for driving wing motion. Dragonflies feature direct flight muscles that attach directly to the wing bases, enabling precise individual [[b1]]. In contrast, advanced species such as flies use indirect musculature that deforms the elastic [[b2]]. When thoracic muscles flex, internal hinges amplify these small movements into [[b3]] wing strokes.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["hesitation", "paralysis", "stagnation", "steering"], "correctAnswer": "steering"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["exoskeleton", "marrow", "skeleton", "tendon"], "correctAnswer": "exoskeleton"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["freezing", "pausing", "stumbling", "sweeping"], "correctAnswer": "sweeping"}]'::jsonb,
     'b1: ''steering'' describes flight control adjustments (''stagnation'', ''paralysis'', and ''hesitation'' denote lack of movement). b2: ''exoskeleton'' is the correct anatomical term for insect outer cuticle (''marrow'', ''skeleton'', and ''tendon'' apply to vertebrate biology). b3: ''sweeping'' describes broad wing stroke arcs (''pausing'', ''stumbling'', and ''freezing'' mean stopping).', 3, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-rwfib', 2, TRUE, 150, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-rwfib-06-03', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-rw', 'Fill in the Blanks (Dropdown)',
     'Asynchronous Physiology: Stretch Activation', 'Below is a text with blanks. Click on each blank, a list of choices will appear. Select the appropriate answer choice for each blank.',
     'In rapidly flapping insects, wingbeat frequencies can exceed one thousand beats per second. Because nerve impulses cannot fire fast enough to signal individual contractions, advanced fliers evolved [[b1]] muscles. In this system, muscle contraction is triggered by mechanical [[b2]] rather than neural commands. A single nerve impulse initiates an oscillating cycle that [[b3]] rapid wing beats with minimal brain overhead.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["asynchronous", "consecutive", "coordinated", "synchronized"], "correctAnswer": "asynchronous"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["compression", "recoil", "slack", "stretch"], "correctAnswer": "stretch"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["halts", "interrupts", "sabotages", "sustains"], "correctAnswer": "sustains"}]'::jsonb,
     'b1: ''asynchronous'' describes muscle oscillations decoupled from direct nerve impulses (''synchronized'', ''consecutive'', and ''coordinated'' mean one-to-one firing). b2: ''stretch'' triggers delayed contraction (''slack'' and ''compression'' mean looseness or squashing; ''recoil'' is rebound). b3: ''sustains'' describes maintaining continuous high frequency (''halts'', ''interrupts'', and ''sabotages'' mean stopping).', 3, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-rwfib', 3, TRUE, 150, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-rwfib-06-04', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-rw', 'Fill in the Blanks (Dropdown)',
     'Vortex Aerodynamics: Leading-Edge Suction', 'Below is a text with blanks. Click on each blank, a list of choices will appear. Select the appropriate answer choice for each blank.',
     'Insect wings achieve remarkable lift by operating at high angles of attack. When an insect sweeps its wing forward, the air separating over the front margin curls into a [[b1]] vortex. This stable vortex creates an area of intense low [[b2]] directly above the wing surface. The resulting suction force produces substantially more lift than could be generated by [[b3]] wings.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["level", "parallel", "spiral", "straight"], "correctAnswer": "spiral"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["drag", "friction", "gravity", "pressure"], "correctAnswer": "pressure"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["chaotic", "mobile", "stationary", "turbulent"], "correctAnswer": "stationary"}]'::jsonb,
     'b1: ''spiral'' describes the leading-edge vortex vortex core (''straight'', ''parallel'', and ''level'' are non-vortical geometry). b2: ''pressure'' (specifically low pressure suction) creates lift (''friction'', ''gravity'', and ''drag'' do not create lift). b3: ''stationary'' matches vortex remaining attached without shedding (''mobile'', ''turbulent'', and ''chaotic'' describe shedding and detachment).', 3, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-rwfib', 4, TRUE, 150, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-rwfib-06-05', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-rw', 'Fill in the Blanks (Dropdown)',
     'Biomimetic Drones: Micro Aerial Robotics', 'Below is a text with blanks. Click on each blank, a list of choices will appear. Select the appropriate answer choice for each blank.',
     'Principles of insect aerodynamics are inspiring the design of miniature robots. Traditional propellers lose efficiency at small scales because air viscosity [[b1]] performance. Consequently, engineers are developing micro aerial vehicles with flapping wings driven by [[b2]] actuators. These compact devices are ideal for navigating hazardous collapsed buildings during [[b3]] operations.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["degrades", "endures", "improves", "thrives"], "correctAnswer": "degrades"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["combustible", "hydraulic", "piezoelectric", "pneumatic"], "correctAnswer": "piezoelectric"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["negligence", "obsolescence", "reconnaissance", "retirement"], "correctAnswer": "reconnaissance"}]'::jsonb,
     'b1: ''degrades'' describes micro-motor efficiency falling at tiny scales (''thrives'', ''improves'', and ''endures'' mean remaining good). b2: ''piezoelectric'' is the precise solid-state actuation technology (''combustible'' means flammable; ''pneumatic'' and ''hydraulic'' are bulky fluid systems). b3: ''reconnaissance'' denotes micro-drone surveillance missions (''negligence'', ''obsolescence'', and ''retirement'' describe failure or discard).', 3, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-rwfib', 5, TRUE, 150, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-rfib-06-01', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-r', 'Reading: Fill in the Blanks',
     'Low Reynolds Physics: Viscous Airflow', 'In the text below some words are missing. Drag words from the box below to the appropriate place in the text. To undo an answer choice, drag the word back to the box below the text.',
     'Operating at millimeter scales fundamentally changes the physics of movement. In the miniature realm of small insects, air acts as a relatively [[b1]] fluid rather than a frictionless gas. Because inertia is low relative to surface [[b2]], wings must actively generate vortex patterns to maintain aerodynamic [[b3]].', '[{"id": "A", "text": "viscous"}, {"id": "B", "text": "drag"}, {"id": "C", "text": "support"}, {"id": "D", "text": "turbulent"}, {"id": "E", "text": "friction"}, {"id": "F", "text": "buoyancy"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["viscous", "drag", "support", "turbulent", "friction", "buoyancy"], "correctAnswer": "viscous"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["viscous", "drag", "support", "turbulent", "friction", "buoyancy"], "correctAnswer": "drag"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["viscous", "drag", "support", "turbulent", "friction", "buoyancy"], "correctAnswer": "support"}]'::jsonb,
     'Air acts as a viscous fluid (thick/resistant), inertia is low relative to drag (friction), requiring vortices for support (lift).', 3, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-rfib', 1, TRUE, 120, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-rfib-06-02', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-r', 'Reading: Fill in the Blanks',
     'Indirect Muscles: Thoracic Oscillation', 'In the text below some words are missing. Drag words from the box below to the appropriate place in the text. To undo an answer choice, drag the word back to the box below the text.',
     'The evolution of indirect flight musculature provided major aerodynamic advantages. Instead of connecting directly to wing joints, massive muscles deform the chitinous [[b1]] of the insect body. The thoracic skeleton functions as a mechanical [[b2]], storing and releasing elastic energy with each wing [[b3]].', '[{"id": "A", "text": "shell"}, {"id": "B", "text": "spring"}, {"id": "C", "text": "stroke"}, {"id": "D", "text": "skeleton"}, {"id": "E", "text": "hinge"}, {"id": "F", "text": "motion"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["shell", "spring", "stroke", "skeleton", "hinge", "motion"], "correctAnswer": "shell"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["shell", "spring", "stroke", "skeleton", "hinge", "motion"], "correctAnswer": "spring"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["shell", "spring", "stroke", "skeleton", "hinge", "motion"], "correctAnswer": "stroke"}]'::jsonb,
     'Muscles deform the outer shell (exoskeleton), which functions as an elastic spring (energy store), with each stroke (flapping cycle).', 3, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-rfib', 2, TRUE, 120, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-rfib-06-03', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-r', 'Reading: Fill in the Blanks',
     'Dynamic Wing Twisting: Stroke Reversal', 'In the text below some words are missing. Drag words from the box below to the appropriate place in the text. To undo an answer choice, drag the word back to the box below the text.',
     'At the conclusion of each half-stroke, insect wings undergo rapid repositioning. Specialized muscles twist the blade to alter its angle before initiating the [[b1]] motion. This rapid rotation generates additional [[b2]], allowing the wing to smoothly cut back through the turbulent [[b3]] left by the previous sweep.', '[{"id": "A", "text": "return"}, {"id": "B", "text": "circulation"}, {"id": "C", "text": "wake"}, {"id": "D", "text": "recovery"}, {"id": "E", "text": "vorticity"}, {"id": "F", "text": "plume"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["return", "circulation", "wake", "recovery", "vorticity", "plume"], "correctAnswer": "return"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["return", "circulation", "wake", "recovery", "vorticity", "plume"], "correctAnswer": "circulation"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["return", "circulation", "wake", "recovery", "vorticity", "plume"], "correctAnswer": "wake"}]'::jsonb,
