    ('q-pr89-rfib-06-04', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-r', 'Reading: Fill in the Blanks',
     'Wake Capture: Energy Recovery', 'In the text below some words are missing. Drag words from the box below to the appropriate place in the text. To undo an answer choice, drag the word back to the box below the text.',
     'Insects utilize sophisticated kinematic maneuvers to conserve energy in flight. When reversing stroke direction, the wing intercepts the swirling vortex [[b1]] behind by the previous movement. By capturing this kinetic energy, the insect produces a powerful pulse of upward [[b2]] while reducing overall muscular [[b3]].', '[{"id": "A", "text": "shed"}, {"id": "B", "text": "thrust"}, {"id": "C", "text": "strain"}, {"id": "D", "text": "formed"}, {"id": "E", "text": "propulsion"}, {"id": "F", "text": "pressure"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["shed", "thrust", "strain", "formed", "propulsion", "pressure"], "correctAnswer": "shed"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["shed", "thrust", "strain", "formed", "propulsion", "pressure"], "correctAnswer": "thrust"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["shed", "thrust", "strain", "formed", "propulsion", "pressure"], "correctAnswer": "strain"}]'::jsonb,
     'The wing intercepts vortices shed previously (released), producing upward thrust (force), reducing muscular strain (effort).', 3, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-rfib', 4, TRUE, 120, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-rfib-06-05', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-r', 'Reading: Fill in the Blanks',
     'Robotic Kinematics: Micro Fabrication', 'In the text below some words are missing. Drag words from the box below to the appropriate place in the text. To undo an answer choice, drag the word back to the box below the text.',
     'Constructing functional robotic insects requires unconventional manufacturing strategies. Traditional mechanical gears and rotary joints cannot easily be [[b1]] at millimeter dimensions. Instead, micro air vehicles rely on flexible carbon-fiber [[b2]] that bend elastically, replicating the resonant movements of biological [[b3]].', '[{"id": "A", "text": "machined"}, {"id": "B", "text": "joints"}, {"id": "C", "text": "wings"}, {"id": "D", "text": "fabricated"}, {"id": "E", "text": "hinges"}, {"id": "F", "text": "blades"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["machined", "joints", "wings", "fabricated", "hinges", "blades"], "correctAnswer": "machined"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["machined", "joints", "wings", "fabricated", "hinges", "blades"], "correctAnswer": "joints"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["machined", "joints", "wings", "fabricated", "hinges", "blades"], "correctAnswer": "wings"}]'::jsonb,
     'Gears cannot be easily machined at micro scales (manufactured), relying on flexible joints (hinges), mimicking wings (appendages).', 3, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-rfib', 5, TRUE, 120, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-single-06-01', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'reading-mcq-single', 'Multiple Choice, Single Answer',
     'Insect Aerodynamics: Steady-State Failure', 'According to paragraph A, why did conventional steady-state aeronautical theory predict that bumblebees could not fly?',
     NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "It assumed insects were too heavy to generate internal metabolic heat"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It failed to recognize that insect flight muscles are powered by hydraulic liquid pressure"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It assumed bumblebee wings were made of porous limestone rather than chitin"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It evaluated wings as static aerofoils, ignoring dynamic unsteady vortex phenomena at low Reynolds numbers"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb,
     'Paragraph A states conventional theory assumed smooth, uninterrupted airflow across rigid, stationary aerofoils, whereas insects operate at low Reynolds numbers in an unsteady aerodynamic regime.', 1, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-single', 1, TRUE, 90, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-single-06-02', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'reading-mcq-single', 'Multiple Choice, Single Answer',
     'Thoracic Anatomy: Indirect Musculature', 'How do indirect flight muscles move the wings in advanced insects (paragraph B)?',
     NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "They pump pressurized blood into hollow wing veins to force downward flapping"}, {"id": "B", "text": "They rotate the insect''s head to deflect incoming atmospheric air currents"}, {"id": "C", "text": "They rhythmically deform the elastic thoracic exoskeleton, which transfers motion to wings through internal lever hinges"}, {"id": "D", "text": "They pull directly on individual tendons anchored to the wing tips"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb,
     'Paragraph B explains that muscles do not attach to wings at all, but rhythmically deform the elastic chitinous exoskeleton, with internal hinges amplifying the deformation into sweeping strokes.', 1, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE Reading', 'Original practice', 'Batch D'],
     'rp-pr89-006', 'g-pr89-006-single', 2, TRUE, 90, NULL),
    ('q-pr89-single-06-03', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'reading-mcq-single', 'Multiple Choice, Single Answer',
